---
scope: src/tools/inspect
status: misaligned
description: Code introspection tool that returns JSDoc, signature, and kind for named symbols without opening full files
intent: >
  aide_inspect gives agents Tier 2 progressive disclosure for code — the ability
  to read a symbol's JSDoc documentation, signature, and kind without opening the
  file or seeing the function body. When an agent knows a function name from an
  import list or orchestrator file, it calls inspect to learn what that function
  does, what it accepts, and what it returns, all from structured metadata rather
  than raw source. This lets agents make informed decisions about which helpers to
  drill into (Tier 3) and which they can use based on the contract alone.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - "Given a symbol name, the tool returns: the symbol's kind (function, method, arrow, class, interface, type alias), file path, line number, the full signature truncated before the body, and parsed JSDoc with description and tags."
    - The tool searches the host project's workspace source files, not the AIDE server's own source.
    - An agent that calls inspect and gets a result back has enough information to decide whether to read the full file or to use the symbol based on its contract alone.
    - The tool handles TypeScript and JavaScript source files across common extensions including ts, tsx, js, jsx, mjs, and cjs.
    - When the named symbol is not found, the tool returns a clear not-found result rather than an error or empty response that leaves the agent guessing.
    - "The MCP tool description registered for `aide_inspect` at server-registration time is an auditable contract surface, not commentary. It accurately states the symbol-lookup contract (returns `kind`, `file`, `line`, `signature`, `jsdoc` per hit — never the function body), the search scope (host project workspace source files, not the AIDE server's own source), the supported file extensions (`ts`, `tsx`, `js`, `jsx`, `mjs`, `cjs`), the optional `file` narrowing parameter, and the Tier 2 progressive-disclosure framing (contract without implementation) — so an agent that reads only the description forms the same expectations it would form by reading this spec. The description, the handler's runtime return shape, and the rest of this spec stay in lockstep; drift between any two of them is a falsifiable QA failure traceable to this outcome."
  undesired:
    - Returning the function body along with the signature. The entire point is Tier 2 — contract without implementation. Including the body defeats progressive disclosure and burns context the agent did not ask for.
    - Silently returning a partial match or a different symbol when the exact name is not found. A wrong match is worse than no match — the agent will act on a contract that does not belong to the symbol it asked about.
    - Requiring the agent to specify a file path to use the tool. The tool should search across workspace files so agents can inspect symbols they know by name but have not yet located.
    - Failing on valid TypeScript constructs like arrow functions assigned to const, exported type aliases, or class methods. These are common patterns in real codebases and a tool that only handles function declarations misses most of them.
    - "A registered MCP tool description for `aide_inspect` that teaches a return field, search scope, supported extension, or behavioral guarantee the handler does not implement or this spec does not define — for example: claiming the tool returns the function body, naming an unsupported file extension, omitting one of the supported extensions, claiming the tool searches the AIDE server's own source instead of the host workspace, claiming the tool throws on not-found instead of returning a structured empty-hits result, or claiming the optional `file` parameter is required. Agents read this description at tool-discovery time and form their Tier 2 mental model from it; teaching the wrong contract there silently misroutes every call regardless of how correct the handler is. Description-vs-handler drift, description-vs-spec drift, and silently-stale description text after a contract change are all the same falsifiable failure."
---
